INT. DERELICT MARS SURFACE HABITAT - COMMON ROOM - SOL 412

Red dust coats every surface. A cracked dome overhead lets in amber light. Cots, a folding table, shelves of dehydrated meals, a dead plant in a coffee tin. A generator whirs weakly in the next compartment.

MISSION SPECIALIST HARRIET "HATTIE" OKONJO-BELL, 40s, in a faded jumpsuit, sits at the table cleaning a helmet visor with a rag. Her hair is chopped short. Her eyes are tired but sharp.

DR. LEOPOLD ASHWORTH, 60s, leans against a shelf, wrapped in a thermal blanket, coughing into a fist. A gray beard has grown unevenly on his cheeks.

COMMS OFFICER RENATA KWIATKOWSKI, 30s, kneels beside a dead radio set with a screwdriver, muttering in Polish.

HATTIE
Any luck?

RENATA
It's the transmitter. The coil's burned through.

HATTIE
Can you fix it?

RENATA
With what? A paper clip and a prayer?

LEOPOLD
(rasping)
I have a prayer.

RENATA
Save it for the oxygen.

Hattie sets the visor down. A faint rumble builds in the walls. The three of them look up. Dust trickles from the dome.

HATTIE
Another quake?

LEOPOLD
No. That's an engine.

Renata drops the screwdriver. They all stare at the ceiling. The rumble grows, a roar, then a shriek of thrusters. A shadow slides across the dome, long and dark.

A final shudder. Silence.

Then a hatch clanks at the far end of the room. It swings open. A figure in a white suit steps through, visor up, face lit by the amber glow: CAPTAIN WILHELMINA "BILLIE" TSANG, 50s, with a rescue beacon in one gloved hand.

BILLIE
Hello, Hattie.

Hattie rises very slowly. Her rag slides from the table.

HATTIE
You came back.

BILLIE
I said I would.

LEOPOLD
(a tired laugh)
You said it forty times.

BILLIE
And I meant it forty times.

Renata crosses the room in three strides and throws her arms around Billie's suit. The visor clacks against her forehead. She laughs and sobs and swears in Polish. Hattie stays where she is, one hand on the back of a chair.

HATTIE
(quietly)
How long do we have?

BILLIE
Eleven minutes before the dust storm.

HATTIE
Then we go.

BILLIE
We go.

She hands the beacon to Leopold, helps him up, drapes the blanket across his shoulders. He leans on her. The generator sputters and stops. In the sudden quiet the dead plant in its coffee tin catches the light from the open hatch, and for one moment, it looks green.
